# 15. Entity Naming Conventions

To maintain consistency, scalability, and compatibility with SQLAlchemy, Alembic, and PostgreSQL best practices, KnowledgeOS follows strict database naming conventions.

---

## 15.1 Naming Convention

All database objects use **lowercase snake_case**.

Examples:

```text id="v8k6pf"
profiles

documents

document_chunks

chat_sessions

user_preferences
```

The same convention applies to:

* Tables
* Columns
* Constraints
* Indexes
* Foreign Keys
* Database Functions
* Triggers

---

## 15.2 Primary Keys

Every application table uses a UUID as its primary key.

```sql
id UUID PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()
```

### Why UUID?

* Globally unique
* Difficult to enumerate
* Distributed-system friendly
* Native PostgreSQL support
* Seamless integration with Supabase

---

## 15.3 Foreign Keys

Every relationship is enforced through foreign key constraints.

Examples include:

```text id="d0mz8a"
profiles.user_id

↓

auth.users.id

documents.folder_id

↓

folders.id

chat_messages.chat_session_id

↓

chat_sessions.id
```

Referential integrity is enforced throughout the schema.

---

## 15.4 Audit Fields

Every mutable entity contains standardized lifecycle timestamps.

```text id="1yxoz6"
created_at

updated_at
```

### created_at

Represents the timestamp at which the record was initially created.

Once written, this value is immutable.

---

### updated_at

Represents the timestamp of the most recent modification.

This value is automatically updated whenever mutable fields change.

The application never manually updates this column.

---

## 15.5 Automatic Timestamp Updates

KnowledgeOS uses PostgreSQL trigger functions to maintain `updated_at`.

A reusable trigger function updates the timestamp before every UPDATE operation.

This trigger is attached to mutable tables including:

* profiles
* folders
* documents
* chat_sessions
* user_preferences
* insights (if editable)

This ensures timestamp consistency regardless of whether changes originate from the application, background jobs, or administrative tools.

---

## 15.6 Soft Delete Convention

Recoverable entities support soft deletion.

Standard fields:

```text id="it5oqf"
is_deleted BOOLEAN

deleted_at TIMESTAMPTZ
```

Deletion follows a two-stage lifecycle:

1. Soft Delete
2. Permanent Delete

During the soft deletion period:

* Records remain physically stored.
* They are hidden from the application.
* AI retrieval excludes them through metadata filtering.

Permanent deletion removes:

* Original files
* Metadata
* Chunks
* ChromaDB vector records

---

## 15.7 Enumerations

Business domains with a limited set of valid values use PostgreSQL ENUM types.

Examples include:

```text id="ph6vjd"
processing_status

document_type

source_type

chat_role

insight_type
```

ENUMs provide:

* Better validation
* Smaller storage footprint
* Improved query readability
* Stronger data integrity

---

## 15.8 Index Naming Convention

Indexes follow a predictable naming pattern.

```text id="0q4kmb"
idx_<table>_<column>

idx_documents_user_id

idx_documents_status

idx_chat_messages_session_id
```

Composite indexes follow:

```text id="o9v3fh"
idx_<table>_<column1>_<column2>
```

---

## 15.9 Constraint Naming Convention

Constraints use descriptive names.

Examples:

```text id="ld28xa"
pk_documents

fk_documents_folder_id

fk_profiles_user_id

uq_profiles_username

chk_processing_status
```

This simplifies debugging and migration management.

---

## 15.10 Trigger Naming Convention

Database triggers follow a consistent naming standard.

```text id="z8h2rm"
trg_update_profiles_timestamp

trg_update_documents_timestamp

trg_update_chat_sessions_timestamp
```

---

## 15.11 ChromaDB Mapping Convention

Every document chunk has a one-to-one conceptual mapping with a vector record stored in ChromaDB.

The `chunk_id` generated in PostgreSQL is reused as the vector record identifier inside ChromaDB.

```text id="t7u2ke"
PostgreSQL

document_chunks.id

↓

ChromaDB

vector_record.id
```

Using the same identifier enables:

* Deterministic synchronization
* Simplified embedding regeneration
* Faster debugging
* Direct citation mapping
* Reliable deletion synchronization

---

## 15.12 Design Principles

The schema follows these core principles:

* Authentication managed exclusively by Supabase Auth.
* Application-specific user data stored in `profiles`.
* UUID primary keys across all entities.
* Strict foreign key enforcement.
* Flat folder hierarchy for the MVP.
* System folders separated from virtual collections.
* Automatic timestamp management through database triggers.
* Immutable original documents.
* Soft deletion before permanent deletion.
* One-to-one conceptual mapping between document chunks and ChromaDB vector records.
* Consistent naming conventions across all database objects.
* Future-proof schema designed for extensibility.

---

# 15.13 Phase Summary

The PostgreSQL schema establishes a modular, secure, and production-oriented foundation for KnowledgeOS.

By separating authentication from application data, enforcing consistent naming conventions, leveraging UUID-based identifiers, automating lifecycle tracking through database triggers, and maintaining deterministic mappings between relational records and vector embeddings, the schema provides a scalable data model capable of supporting current features and future platform expansion.

